{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex; cat paper_strategy.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\n\\title{Grounded Experimental Summary}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nReplace with a concise grounded abstract summarizing setup, core result, and takeaway.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nReplace with grounded motivation, problem framing, and contributions.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\nSummarize relevant prior work using citation keys present in references.bib (for example, \\citep{templateReference2026}).\n\n\\section{Method}\nDescribe data, setup, and method details needed for reproducibility.\n\n\\section{Results}\nReport grounded quantitative and qualitative findings. Include figures and tables when useful.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nState key findings, limitations, and next steps supported by evidence.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n% REQUIRED: Replace this scaffold with substantive grounded content.\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Details}\nReplace with grounded extended methods, reproducibility details, additional analyses, or supporting tables and figures.\n\n\\end{document}\n# Paper Framing Strategy\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status:** ready for a bounded standalone empirical-methodology paper, not a claim of a new optimizer, new post-selection theorem, or universal improvement. Selected family: `paper_standalone`; writing skill: `write-paper-standalone`.\n\n**Provisional title:** Protected Reporting After Prompt Selection: Benefits and Limits\n\n**Central question:** When does reserving benchmark questions for reporting reduce error after prompt selection, and what can known-truth simulations versus finite real-model audits establish about reliability?\n\n**Central thesis:** Across the five final seeds, half-split reporting lowers mean error relative to reusing the same winner's selection score in the sixteen-prompt conditions, but does not uniformly beat full-budget or non-search comparators, and the real-model results establish audit agreement rather than population validity.\n\nThe contribution is a controlled, explicitly limited comparison of reporting targets, selection multiplicity, and reporting-sample cost. Sample splitting itself is conventional. Do not claim that the search invented it or that reliable reporting necessarily increases selected-prompt accuracy. Simulation and real inference serve different evidential roles under one question, not separate chronological experiments.\n\n## Evidence and Framing Alternatives\n\nRead the independent final record before interpreting the search. `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, and `final.qualification.correct` report **completed / qualified / true**. This run does not have a failed final qualification. That status establishes neither statistical superiority nor general scientific validity. Candidate diagnostics in `final.evidence.episodes` remain **unverified**; missing detailed gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n\nSeparately, `study.selection_kind` retains the original baseline; `study.investigation_outcome` reports no qualifying replacement. `study.attempts.scored` is zero, `study.attempts.unscored` is three, and `study.search_score_delta` is zero. Unscored attempts are missing performance evidence, not measured losses. Search scores are not benchmark accuracies and cannot be compared with final-scope metrics as improvements.\n\nNo resolved edit-context files, prior manuscript, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` are present. The JSON decision is **recentered**: from the broad task ambition and private proposals to the retained policy's measured reporting trade-off, not a replacement of an existing manuscript.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve a broad automatic-tuning success story | Reject: no scored replacement or measured search improvement. There is no prior manuscript to preserve. |\n| Broaden to paired certificates, allocation variants, or an adaptive method family | Reject: no final measurements of those interventions; unscored candidates do not support efficacy claims. |\n| Recenter on the frozen-bank reporting trade-off | Select: directly implemented policy, matched reporting comparison, known-truth conditions, real inference, and explicit counterexamples. |\n| Lead with universal superiority of protected reporting | Reject: full-budget and fixed-prompt aggregate MSE are lower; comparison targets and observation budgets differ. |\n| Reject solely because the campaign found no winner | Reject: the independent completed final evaluation supports a bounded empirical study of the retained method. |\n\nThe selected and original-baseline program sources are byte-identical. Treat the baseline as the studied policy, not as a separate identical comparator. `task/evaluate_final.py` has the exact SHA256 recorded in the final evaluator metadata, providing direct linkage for inspected estimator and control definitions. The program-tree hashes reported in different scopes differ; do not silently equate those scopes or claim a stronger source-tree identity than the evidence establishes.\n\n## Methods Worth Including\n\n1. **Protected half-split reporting — studied policy.** Frozen sixteen-prompt bank, seeded random half selection/half reporting, uniform report weights, fixed tie order, 95% Wilson interval. Select the maximum-accuracy prompt on selection rows. Do not call this a novel estimator.\n2. **Same-winner selection-score reuse — primary baseline.** Report the selected prompt's selection-row mean. The winner is identical, so this comparison isolates which rows produce the reported score; it is not full-benchmark reuse.\n3. **Full-budget selection and reuse — budget-aware baseline.** Select and report using all available questions. Its winner can differ. This is a practical trade-off, not a pure reporting ablation.\n4. **Fixed and random prompts — non-search baselines.** Use all questions and evaluate each method against its own prompt target. Lower reporting MSE does not imply higher prompt accuracy.\n5. **Alternative half split and controls — supporting only.** The evaluator's first-half/second-half split is another implementation of the same statistical idea. Constant, copied-arm, contaminated-report, permutation, and replay checks are diagnostics, not new performance methods.\n\nExclude unmeasured certificates, outcome corrections, quarter allocations, stratum-balanced performance claims, new architectures, and generic optimizer comparisons. There are no scored ablation studies to turn into an ablation narrative.\n\n## Results That Carry the Argument\n\nThe final evidence contains five seeds, each with eight conditions: synthetic/real, total questions 64/256, and prompts 1/16. Synthetic conditions include four regimes with 1024 replicates each; real conditions have one realized benchmark/audit comparison per seed, not 1024 model runs. Use `final.evidence.episodes` for condition-level comparisons and uncertainty.\n\n**Primary comparison.** Show protected reporting versus same-winner selection reuse and full-budget reuse, stratified by evidence family, question budget, and prompt count. The sixteen-prompt condition means support lower protected-report error than same-winner reuse. The one-prompt condition removes selection multiplicity and reveals that withholding observations still costs precision. Synthetic full-budget reporting has lower MSE than protected reporting in the condition means; keep that counterexample visible.\n\n**Secondary aggregate context.** Supplied aggregate MSE is 0.0033498776393466522 for protected reporting and 0.005354862639043066 for selection reuse. Full-budget reuse is 0.002705803510381116 and fixed-prompt reporting is 0.0021956944465637207. These come from `final.metric.report_mse`, `final.metric.reuse_mse`, `final.metric.full_budget_naive_mse`, and `final.metric.fixed_prompt_mse`. They support a trade-off, not a universal ranking. Equal weighting across conditions and synthetic regimes is not pooling all simulation replicates and real questions into one population estimate.\n\n**Known-truth evidence.** Explain the four synthetic regimes: equal/graded prompt means crossed with shared-outcome mixing parameters 0.1/0.9. Do not mislabel that mixing parameter as an exact Pearson correlation. Retained regime metrics support bias, optimism, MSE, Wilson coverage, width, and report-MSE standard-error summaries. A compact main table should expose these mechanisms; complete regime tables belong in the supplement. Simulation coverage is not a new inferential theorem.\n\n**Real evidence.** Runtime and split manifests identify CPU float32 `HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM2-135M-Instruct`, revision `12fd25f77366fa6b3b4b768ec3050bf629380bac`, and MMLU-Pro revision `b189ec765aa7ed75c8acfea42df31fdae71f97be`. Generation is deterministic and constrained to one legal answer-letter token; context exclusions and invalid outputs count as errors. Do not substitute an assumed larger model or free-form reasoning pipeline.\n\nEach real target is a **512-question finite audit**, not known fresh-population accuracy. The supplied paired subject-bootstrap interval estimates **selected-minus-fixed audit accuracy**, using 200 bootstrap draws; it is not an interval for reporting optimism or an MSE difference. Plot all five seeds in both sixteen-prompt question-budget conditions, including zero-crossing and negative estimates. Do not select only positive intervals. Shared-pool sampling and overlapping conditions preclude interpreting these as independent datasets or pooling their intervals into a blanket significance claim. Public-data pretraining contamination is possible, not demonstrated.\n\n**Validity boundary.** Deliberately copied reporting rows correctly record failed independence in the contaminated control; that is not the retained policy failing final qualification. Completed controls and replay do not certify representative sampling, adaptation-safe inference, or population coverage. Preserve unverified diagnostics in the body. If an authoritative later record changes qualification to `not_qualified`, disclose it in writing, review, audit, and revision without inventing a statistical, validity, or adaptivity cause.\n\nThe recorded decision-reversal quantity compares protected-report-minus-full-sample-fixed and selected-target-minus-fixed-target signs, excluding ties. It is not directly the probability that a naive winner claim gets overturned. Omit it from the central narrative unless this precise definition is necessary.\n\n## Required Figures and Layout\n\n`paper.sty` sets **one text column, 5.5 inches wide and 9 inches high**. Consequently full-width and column-width mean the same full text span here, not a two-column float. Do not rescue dense plots by shrinking them. The JSON specs provide canonical generator, grouping, sources, and readability budgets.\n\n| Figure | Design and role | Placement and readability budget |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `reporting_overview` | Concept diagram: bank, selection/report split, same-winner reuse, and known-truth versus finite-audit targets; distinguish the different-winner full-budget comparator. Use `generate-concept-figure`, with editable draw.io and prompt provenance. | Page 1 or 2; full 5.5-inch width, about 1.6 inches high plus caption, roughly one fifth page. One panel, at most six semantic blocks, three paths/series, at least 9-point final labels. No numerical decoration or adaptive feedback loop. |\n| `reporting_error_comparison` | Matplotlib 2x2 grouped-point comparison: rows synthetic/real, columns question budget; K=1/16 categories, three reporting series, and five seed dots. Label the synthetic row population MSE and real row audit-reference squared error. | Full 5.5-inch width, about 2.8 inches high plus caption, roughly two fifths page. Four panels, two per row, two categories and three series per panel. One legend, at least 8.5-point final labels. Match scales within rows or make differences unmistakable. |\n| `real_audit_accuracy_difference` | Matplotlib horizontal paired-bootstrap intervals, selected minus fixed audit accuracy at K=16; two question-budget panels with every final seed and a zero line. | Full 5.5-inch width, about 1.8 inches high plus caption, roughly one quarter page. Two panels in one row, five seed categories, one series; shared x limits and at least 8.5-point labels. |\n\nSeed dots are within method series, not five extra legend series. Use descriptive means/scatter, not invented error bars from unavailable paired replicate data. Include a compact main table with all six reporting comparators and another compact synthetic regime diagnostic table covering bias, reuse optimism, coverage and width. Do not turn the table into a winner leaderboard.\n\nQuantitative plots must have reproducible source, **vector PDF plus 300-DPI PNG**, and LaTeX must use PDF when available. Raster-only full-width assets must be at least 2400 pixels wide; column insets at least 1200 pixels. Regenerate from source rather than upscale. Exactly one planned overview uses `generate-concept-figure`; no figure is generated at this stage.\n\n## Body Budget and Venue Checks\n\nTarget **8.5 body pages**, within the venue's 8–9-page requirement, excluding references. An exactly eight-page body must fill at least 75% of its final page. Budget allocations are plans, not experimental measurements:\n\n| Content | Pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Title, abstract, problem, concrete contributions, early overview | 1.25 |\n| Related work | 0.75 |\n| Estimands and reporting policies | 1.50 |\n| Experimental design and provenance | 1.00 |\n| Controlled comparisons, Figure 2, all-comparator table and regime diagnosis | 2.00 |\n| Real audit uncertainty and Figure 3 | 0.75 |\n| Interpretation, limitations, reproducibility pointer and conclusion | 1.25 |\n\nUse the space for target definitions, budget fairness, dependence, uncertainty, and explicit counterexamples; not filler, oversized graphics, or invented additional experiments. Suggested headings are **Introduction; Related Work; Selection and Reporting Targets; Reporting Policies; Experimental Design; Reporting Error and Selection Multiplicity; Finite-Audit Evidence; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion**.\n\n`VENUE.md` mandates **ICLR 2026**, double-blind anonymity, and a separate **LLM Usage** section. The staged resource template is named `iclr2027_conference` and the working style has historical comments. The matching geometry is inspected, but official 2026 provenance is not established. The downstream writer must verify or obtain the correct official 2026 files through a direct known official source, not silently relabel the 2027 template. Omit author identities, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements and identifying links; do not alter geometry to force page fit.\n\n## Supplementary Information\n\nPlace it in the same manuscript **after references**: clear page, bibliography, clear page, appendix, `Supplementary Information` section. Keep essential counterexamples, audit limitations, and diagnostic caveats in the body. Separately supplied appendix-ranked analyses belong here; this strategy does not guess their ranking.\n\n1. **Complete Condition and Regime Results.** All eight conditions for each seed; all six comparator metrics; full synthetic regime summaries and supplied report-MSE standard errors. Distinguish population coverage from real audit containment. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.report_mse`, `final.metric.reuse_mse`, `final.metric.honest_half_mse`, `final.metric.fixed_prompt_mse`, `final.metric.random_prompt_mse`, `final.metric.full_budget_naive_mse`.\n2. **Partition, Estimand, and Uncertainty Details.** Exact prompt bank, tie order, random partition, alternative half split, Wilson formula, each method's target, synthetic construction, averaging, and subject-bootstrap semantics, using the SHA256-matched evaluator and staged source. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `study.selection_kind`, `final.metric.coverage`, `final.metric.interval_width`.\n3. **Diagnostic Controls and Evidence Boundaries.** Constant/copy/permutation/replay observations, deliberate contaminated-report independence failure, unverified diagnostics, unknown gate checks, and hash-scope distinctions. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, `final.qualification.correct`, `final.metric.control_failure_rate`, `final.metric.replay_discrepancy`.\n4. **Reproducibility, Resources, and Incomplete Comparisons.** Local model/data manifests, dependency versions, seeds, commands and eventual figure/table scripts; observed episode durations rather than invented CPU-hours or token counts. Distinguish future reproduction commands from completed execution. Disclose baseline retention and three unscored attempts without guessing timeout causes. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `study.selection_kind`, `study.investigation_outcome`, `study.attempts.scored`, `study.attempts.unscored`, `study.search_score_delta`.\n5. **LLM Usage.** Precisely disclose observed assistance in ideation, implementation, reference planning and writing, distinct from evaluated answer generation. Do not invent human oversight or use identifying internal paths. Sources: `study.selection_kind`, `study.attempts.total`, `final.evidence.episodes`; current workflow provenance supports the writing disclosure, not numerical scientific claims.\n\n## Wording and Grounding Guardrails\n\nAvoid headings such as **Experiment A / Experiment B; Search Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones and First Success; Round-by-Round Improvements; Winning Configuration; From Failed Attempts to a Final Breakthrough**.\n\nAvoid phrases such as “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “next-best,” “later variants,” “our search discovered a superior method,” “three failed methods performed worse,” “guaranteed unbiased reporting,” “validated population coverage on MMLU-Pro,” and “uniformly outperforms all baselines.” The exact forbidden internal-label list is in JSON; do not expose those labels in reader-facing text or figure legends. No threshold-rate figure is needed.\n\nUse `final.metric.*` and `final.evidence.episodes` for results, `final.qualification.*` for final status, and `study.*` for selection/search accounting. Private memory supplies no quantitative evidence. A bibliography entry and metadata provenance do not establish that an original document was consulted or that a paper is relevant. Verify consultation and cite only relevant actual keys; do not pad related work with incidental retrieval matches or invent missing benchmark, Wilson, or bootstrap references. The permitted starter exact-title scope and `Berk2013ValidPI` are positioning leads, not a claim of completed reading. Do not discover, identify, search for, or follow citation graphs to the prohibited paper or related discussion. Stop and log any known accidental encounter. This framing agent performed no external literature browsing.\n\nThe strategy is ready because the evidence supports a coherent, narrow study, **not** because novelty, conference acceptance, full reproducibility, or general validity is established. The template-year and citation-consultation checks remain explicit downstream production requirements. This stage writes no final manuscript, claim ledger, figures, new experiments, or final-paper validation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; ls .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex; cat paper_strategy.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; ls .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex; cat paper_strategy.json'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\npaper.bst\npaper.sty\npaper.tex\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\n\\title{Grounded Experimental Summary}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nReplace with a concise grounded abstract summarizing setup, core result, and takeaway.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nReplace with grounded motivation, problem framing, and contributions.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\nSummarize relevant prior work using citation keys present in references.bib (for example, \\citep{templateReference2026}).\n\n\\section{Method}\nDescribe data, setup, and method details needed for reproducibility.\n\n\\section{Results}\nReport grounded quantitative and qualitative findings. Include figures and tables when useful.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nState key findings, limitations, and next steps supported by evidence.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n% REQUIRED: Replace this scaffold with substantive grounded content.\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Details}\nReplace with grounded extended methods, reproducibility details, additional analyses, or supporting tables and figures.\n\n\\end{document}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Protected Reporting After Prompt Selection: Benefits and Limits\",\n  \"central_question\": \"When does reserving benchmark questions for reporting reduce error after prompt selection, and what can known-truth simulations versus finite real-model audits establish about reliability?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"Across the five final seeds, half-split reporting lowers mean error relative to reusing the same winner's selection score in the sixteen-prompt conditions, but does not uniformly beat full-budget or non-search comparators, and the real-model results establish audit agreement rather than population validity.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is a controlled study of a retained, conventional reporting policy, not an improved optimizer or a new inference method. Known-truth synthetic conditions isolate selection optimism and the cost of withholding observations; a small real instruction model supplies a bounded empirical check with explicit audit uncertainty. The final evaluation is independently completed and qualified, even though the search found no qualifying replacement and all three candidate attempts were unscored. Method novelty, universal superiority, and reliable fresh-population coverage are not supported. A standalone empirical-methodology paper is coherent if it leads with the comparison and keeps these counterexamples and limitations in the main text.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"recentered\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"No previous_paper directory, resolved edit-context file, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is present; this is fresh framing, not a manuscript-preservation decision. The recentered label denotes a move from the task's broad automatic-tuning ambition and private proposal history to the frozen baseline's measured reporting trade-off. There is no prior manuscript title or figure plan to preserve.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"The staged selected and baseline source files are byte-identical and implement a frozen sixteen-prompt bank, seeded random half selection/half reporting, uniform reporting weights, and fixed tie priority. This rules out treating their comparison as an intervention or gain.\",\n    \"study.selection_kind, study.investigation_outcome, study.search_score_delta, and study.attempts.* establish baseline retention, no qualifying search replacement, zero search-score delta, zero scored attempts, and three unscored attempts. The missing scores are not losses.\",\n    \"final.qualification.status/outcome/correct independently establish completed/qualified/true. final.evidence.episodes contains five seeds and eight conditions per seed; this final evidence, not the search outcome, enables the paper.\",\n    \"The staged task/evaluate_final.py SHA256 exactly matches final_evaluation.evaluator_sha256. Its estimator, controls, bootstrap target, synthetic construction, and aggregation therefore have direct evaluator linkage rather than speculative family linkage.\",\n    \"The runtime and split manifests identify CPU float32 SmolLM2-135M-Instruct with deterministic one-letter constrained decoding and pinned MMLU-Pro. They narrow the real evidence to one small model and one public benchmark, not arbitrary LLM pipelines.\",\n    \"The retained episode details mark candidate diagnostics unverified. Intentional contaminated-copy independence flags must be explained as negative controls, not mistaken for the actual policy failing final qualification; they also cannot establish general validity or adaptivity robustness.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Protected half-split reporting\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Central studied policy: select the highest-accuracy prompt on one random half and report its uniform accuracy with a 95% Wilson interval on the disjoint half. It is the retained baseline, not a newly invented estimator.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Same-winner selection-score reuse\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Primary matched comparison: the selected prompt is identical to that of protected reporting, isolating the reporting sample rather than a different winner. Do not call this full-benchmark reuse.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Full-budget selection and reuse\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Select and report on all available benchmark questions. This uses a different winner and more reporting observations, exposing the bias-variance and selection-budget trade-off rather than providing a pure reporting ablation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Fixed and random prompt reporting\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Non-search reference points with full-question reporting; each error is evaluated against that method's own prompt target. They can have lower reporting MSE without selecting a better prompt. Include in the main numerical table, not as extra series in crowded plots.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Alternative half-split and diagnostic controls\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The evaluator's first-half/second-half comparator is another half-split implementation, not a distinct statistical advance. Constant outputs, copied arms, deliberately contaminated reporting, permutation, and replay check protocol behavior. Explain their limited roles; they are not outcome-adaptive methods or proof of population validity.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Separate the scientific condition grid from campaign history: synthetic versus real evidence, total question budget n in {64,256}, and prompt count K in {1,16}, each over five final seeds. Synthetic details contain four regimes with 1024 replicates each; real details contain one benchmark/audit realization per seed and condition, not 1024 independent model runs.\",\n    \"Lead with condition-specific protected-report, same-winner-reuse, and full-budget-reuse error comparisons. Use the single-prompt condition as a no-selection diagnostic: splitting still costs observations. Keep the synthetic full-budget advantage visible rather than asserting that reduced optimism guarantees lower MSE.\",\n    \"Use the supplied aggregate only as secondary context: final.metric.report_mse=0.0033498776393466522 versus final.metric.reuse_mse=0.005354862639043066, but final.metric.full_budget_naive_mse=0.002705803510381116 and final.metric.fixed_prompt_mse=0.0021956944465637207 are lower. Do not conflate this mixed-family average with population risk or a single dataset result.\",\n    \"Explain synthetic optimism using equal versus graded arm means and shared-outcome mixing parameters 0.1 versus 0.9; the latter is the generator's dependence parameter, not an asserted exact Pearson correlation. Use regime-wise bias, MSE, Wilson coverage and width from episode details. Known truth supports simulation coverage statements, not a new coverage theorem.\",\n    \"For real data, report error relative to a 512-question audit and selected-minus-fixed audit accuracy with the retained paired subject-bootstrap intervals. These intervals concern the accuracy difference, not reporting bias or the MSE difference. Do not portray whether a Wilson interval contains an audit estimate as known population coverage.\",\n    \"Make the lack of stable real-model superiority explicit: the retained sixteen-prompt audit intervals have mixed signs/zero inclusion, and the conditions share a public data pool. Show every final seed, not only intervals above zero. Do not pool these correlated intervals into an unqualified significance claim.\",\n    \"Keep source-qualified status separate from scientific conclusions: final.qualification.* reports a qualified completed evaluation; study.* reports no scored candidate improvement. Missing detailed gate diagnostics and unverified candidate diagnostics remain limitations despite that status.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early conceptual overview: frozen prompt bank, random selection/report split, same-winner reuse alternative, and separate synthetic-known-truth versus finite-real-audit targets; mark full-budget selection as a different winner/comparison.\",\n    \"Figure 2, main direct comparison: a two-by-two condition grid, with rows synthetic/real and columns n=64/256, two K categories and three reporting series; overlay five seed-level observations and distinguish population MSE from audit-reference squared error in the row labels.\",\n    \"Figure 3, real uncertainty: two horizontal interval panels for n=64/256, displaying selected-minus-fixed audit accuracy for all five final seeds at K=16, with zero reference and retained paired subject-bootstrap intervals.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"reporting_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Give immediate glance value for the estimand, protected sample, same-winner comparator, and unequal evidential strength of known truth and finite audit.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"reporting_policy\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; conceptual evidence and data-flow map\",\n      \"split_by\": \"selection/report data roles and synthetic/real targets\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"paper.sty sets a single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height. Use that full 5.5-inch span on page 1 or 2, approximately 1.6 inches high plus caption (about one fifth page), with at least 9-point final labels. A half-width inset would compress branching data roles; there is no two-column figure* assumption.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"study.selection_kind\"],\n      \"implementation_notes\": \"At most six semantic blocks, short node labels, no numerical result decorations, no invented model architecture or adaptive feedback loop. Describe selection/report independence as the intended random-partition design, not an evaluator-certified population property. Require editable draw.io output and regenerate rather than shrink if text exceeds budget.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"reporting_error_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Compare error while keeping sample size, prompt count, target type, and the strongest counterexample to uniform superiority visible.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_point_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"prompt count K=1 or K=16\",\n      \"metric\": \"synthetic: mean squared error to known selected-prompt accuracy; real: squared discrepancy to audit accuracy\",\n      \"split_by\": \"synthetic versus real and n=64 versus n=256\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use 5.5 inches by approximately 2.8 inches plus caption, a 2x2 grid with only two x categories and three series per panel. A single legend and at least 8.5-point rendered labels keep it readable at full text width; half-width is unsuitable. Reserve about two fifths page. Use matched scales within each evidence row or explicitly label any row-specific scale; do not imply identical targets across rows.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 4,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 2,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.report_mse\", \"final.metric.reuse_mse\", \"final.metric.full_budget_naive_mse\"],\n      \"implementation_notes\": \"Three series: protected report, selection-score reuse, full-budget reuse. Overlay seed points within those series rather than adding seed legend entries. Means are descriptive across the five retained seeds, with synthetic regimes equally weighted. Do not invent paired-difference standard errors from marginal MSE standard errors; display seed scatter instead. Use a table for the remaining comparators.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"real_audit_accuracy_difference\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Prevent improved reporting from being mistaken for improved selected-prompt accuracy and expose finite-audit uncertainty.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"horizontal_interval_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"final evaluation seed\",\n      \"metric\": \"selected minus fixed prompt audit accuracy, with paired subject-bootstrap 95% percentile interval\",\n      \"split_by\": \"n=64 versus n=256 at K=16\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use a 5.5-inch-wide two-panel plot approximately 1.8 inches high plus caption (roughly one quarter page), five horizontal seed rows per panel and one interval series. Keep at least 8.5-point labels, shared x limits, and a zero line. Full-width avoids squeezing signed accuracy labels into a narrow inset; in this single-column style full-width equals column width.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1,\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"implementation_notes\": \"Point estimates equal detail.target minus detail.fixed[1]; endpoints are detail.paired_subject_bootstrap_interval. All five seeds must appear. The evaluator uses 200 subject-cluster bootstrap draws, and these intervals are not simultaneous or a validation of the bootstrap procedure. K=1 yields the identical prompt and is tabulated rather than using a redundant panel.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"supplementary_plan\": {\n    \"purpose\": \"Provide full condition and regime accountability, exact statistical semantics, and reproducibility beyond the main comparison, without relocating the central counterexamples or essential validity limitations out of the body.\",\n    \"planned_subsections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Complete Condition and Regime Results\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Tabulate all eight conditions for each of the five final seeds, all six reporting comparators, selected accuracy, report bias, reuse optimism, interval width, and coverage indicators. Expand synthetic results into four regimes per condition using the retained metrics and report-MSE standard errors. Separate real audit-containment indicators from synthetic population coverage; do not estimate unobserved replicate distributions.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.report_mse\", \"final.metric.reuse_mse\", \"final.metric.honest_half_mse\", \"final.metric.fixed_prompt_mse\", \"final.metric.random_prompt_mse\", \"final.metric.full_budget_naive_mse\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Partition, Estimand, and Uncertainty Details\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Specify frozen prompt texts and tie order, seeded half partition, the evaluator's alternative half split, Wilson interval calculation, own-winner targets for differing selection methods, synthetic shared-uniform generator and arm means, equal weighting, and the 200-draw subject bootstrap for selected-minus-fixed audit accuracy. Ground definitions in the SHA256-matched task/evaluate_final.py and staged policy source; do not present these as newly proved inferential guarantees.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"study.selection_kind\", \"final.metric.coverage\", \"final.metric.interval_width\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Diagnostic Controls and Evidence Boundaries\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document constant, copy-arm, deliberately contaminated copy-report, effective permutation, and replay observations. Explain why the contaminated control records independence=false while final qualification is qualified. State that candidate diagnostics are unverified and unknown gate checks cannot be inferred. Describe the unmatched program-tree hash scopes without asserting identity across those scopes; selected versus baseline local source equality is directly established.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\", \"final.metric.control_failure_rate\", \"final.metric.replay_discrepancy\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Reproducibility, Resources, and Incomplete Comparisons\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Record model and dataset revisions from task/resources/final/runtime_manifest.json and split_manifest.json, CPU precision, constrained decoding, context exclusions, dependencies from task/requirements.txt, seed list and evaluator command flags. Include per-seed observed durations from final evidence, distinguishing elapsed evaluator time from unrecorded CPU-hours, inference calls, and tokens. Give future reproduction/table/figure generation commands as instructions, not already executed experiments. Transparently note the retained baseline, three unscored search attempts, and absent scored ablations; do not narrate speculative reasons for timeouts or call them measured losses.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"study.selection_kind\", \"study.investigation_outcome\", \"study.attempts.scored\", \"study.attempts.unscored\", \"study.search_score_delta\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose task-observed LLM assistance in research ideation, policy implementation, reference planning, and manuscript framing/writing separately from the evaluated model's benchmark-answer generation. Precisely distinguish retained evidence from proposals and do not infer undocumented human oversight or verification. This is mandatory under VENUE.md and must not contain author-identifying paths or internal service names.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"study.attempts.total\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"forbidden_headings\": [\n    \"Experiment A / Experiment B\",\n    \"Search Progression\",\n    \"Campaign Phases\",\n    \"Milestones and First Success\",\n    \"Round-by-Round Improvements\",\n    \"Winning Configuration\",\n    \"From Failed Attempts to a Final Breakthrough\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_phrases\": [\n    \"we then tried\",\n    \"first valid result\",\n    \"subsequent improvements\",\n    \"next-best\",\n    \"later variants\",\n    \"our search discovered a superior method\",\n    \"three failed methods performed worse\",\n    \"guaranteed unbiased reporting\",\n    \"validated population coverage on MMLU-Pro\",\n    \"uniformly outperforms all baselines\",\n    \"correctness rate\",\n    \"correct and eligible\",\n    \"eligible\",\n    \"combined_score\",\n    \"rubric\",\n    \"worker_01\",\n    \"worker_02\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_writer\": [\n    \"Ground quantitative claims in final.metric.* or final.evidence.episodes, qualification in final.qualification.*, and selection, search differences, and attempt accounting in study.*. Never substitute downstream_study.final_evaluation for the independent final record. If authoritative qualification changes to not_qualified, disclose it in the body, review, audit and revision; identify a cause only if evaluator evidence establishes it. The current source says qualified, not failed.\",\n    \"Private-only pipeline context: the retained search score equals its baseline score and has zero delta; these are not accuracy measurements or final-scope improvements. Do not put those internal score labels into visible figures. The baseline is the studied policy; do not manufacture a second identical baseline series.\",\n    \"Problem formalization should distinguish a prompt selected from a frozen bank, its conditional fresh-question accuracy, the reused selection mean, the protected report mean, and the finite audit estimate. Reporting MSE and selected-prompt accuracy answer different questions. Full-budget, fixed, random, and alternative-half methods have their own prompt targets, so their MSE differences do not isolate a single mechanism.\",\n    \"The real setting is HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM2-135M-Instruct revision 12fd25f77366fa6b3b4b768ec3050bf629380bac on MMLU-Pro revision b189ec765aa7ed75c8acfea42df31fdae71f97be, CPU float32, deterministic constrained one-letter generation. Do not replace it with an assumed 7B model, chain-of-thought optimizer, DSPy implementation, or free-form response task. Public-data pretraining contamination is possible, not established.\",\n    \"The four synthetic regimes cross equal/graded means and shared-outcome dependence; one prompt has no selection multiplicity. All source-defined conditions remain visible. The cross-family aggregate uses equal case/seed/regime weights rather than pooling synthetic replicates with real examples. Derive any display summaries reproducibly from paper_data.json and label weighting explicitly; do not introduce ungrounded numeric confidence intervals.\",\n    \"Observed control completion, zero replay discrepancy, and qualified status do not imply representative sampling, verified candidate diagnostics, adaptation-safe inference, or reliable repeated-population coverage. The copy-report control is deliberately contaminated and correctly records false independence. The decision-reversal metric uses protected-report-minus-full-sample-fixed versus selected-target-minus-fixed-target signs, excludes ties, and is not the probability that a naive claim is overturned; omit it from the main story unless its exact estimand is explained.\",\n    \"Use one common research question with separate evidential roles: known-truth simulation for bias/coverage and real inference for finite-audit agreement and prompt-accuracy uncertainty. Do not merge them into a single population coverage headline. Real audit pools overlap across settings and derive from a shared public pool; seed scatter is descriptive, not evidence for independent datasets or repeated training runs.\",\n    \"Body target is 8.5 pages within VENUE.md's 8-9-page limit, excluding references: 1.25 pages for title/abstract/problem/contributions and the early overview; 0.75 related work; 1.5 estimands and reporting policies; 1.0 design/provenance; 2.0 controlled comparisons with Figure 2 and the all-comparator table; 0.75 real audit uncertainty with Figure 3; 1.25 interpretation, limitations, reproducibility pointer and conclusion. These are layout allocations, not measured results. A body of exactly 8 pages requires its last page at least 75% full. Spend space on target distinctions, regime-wise uncertainty and counterexamples rather than larger figures, repeated prose or new experiments.\",\n    \"Suggested body headings: Introduction; Related Work; Selection and Reporting Targets; Reporting Policies; Experimental Design; Reporting Error and Selection Multiplicity; Finite-Audit Evidence; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Include a compact main table of all six comparators and a synthetic regime diagnostic table covering report bias, reuse optimism, coverage and interval width. The negative comparisons and finite-audit caveat remain in the body, not only the supplement.\",\n    \"Inspect and retain paper.sty's practical single-column geometry: 5.5 inches text width by 9 inches height. In this format column-width and full-width both span the one text column; the full_width figure designation means that span, not a two-column float. The local style has historical comments, and the staged task template is named iclr2027_conference while VENUE.md mandates ICLR 2026. Do not silently advertise the 2027 template as official 2026. Before final drafting/format validation, verify the required 2026 style provenance or obtain the exact official 2026 files through a direct known official source without prohibited-literature discovery; preserve anonymization and do not change geometry to force fit.\",\n    \"Place Supplementary Information after references in the same manuscript using clearpage, bibliography, clearpage, appendix, section Supplementary Information. Include the mandatory LLM Usage subsection, substantive full results and reproduction details. Route separately supplied appendix-ranked analyses there; do not guess or modify their ranking here.\",\n    \"Quantitative figures must be reproducible from local plotting source, exported as vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI, with LaTeX referencing PDF whenever available. At full 5.5-inch span use at least 2400-pixel width for raster-only assets; any column inset uses at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale existing raster output. Retain editable concept-figure source and prompt provenance; at most one overview uses generate-concept-figure. Record final dimensions, placement and minimum rendered font in the later figure manifest.\",\n    \"References are a bibliography/provenance pool, not evidence that documents were read or that retrieved items are relevant. Do not pad related work with incidental retrieval matches. The permitted starter exact-title scope and retrieved Berk2013ValidPI provide positioning leads only after original-document consultation is verified; never invent missing MMLU-Pro, Wilson or bootstrap citations. Do not search for the forbidden paper, its identity, citation graph, summaries, or reproductions; stop and log any known accidental encounter. No external literature browsing was performed by this framing agent.\",\n    \"Do not claim paired certificates, outcome-dependent correction, quarter allocation, stratum-balanced gains, adaptive-search guarantees, unseen models, theoretical novelty, or completed ablations. Those are not measured interventions in the final evidence. No new inference, simulations, final paper, claim ledger, or final-paper validation belongs in this stage.\",\n    \"Ready means the artifacts support a bounded standalone strategy, not that they establish main-conference novelty, acceptance, statistical superiority, or completed reproduction. The template-year mismatch and precise citation consultation are explicit downstream production checks, not permission to dilute the scientific limitations.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
