{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_117","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/paper-tools/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport pymupdf\npdf=pymupdf.open('paper.pdf')\npage=pdf[7]; spans=[span for block in page.get_text('dict')['blocks'] if 'lines' in block for line in block['lines'] for span in line['spans'] if span['bbox'][0]>=105 and span['bbox'][1]>=60 and span['bbox'][3]<=730]\nprint('Page 8 body bounds',min(s['bbox'][1] for s in spans), max(s['bbox'][3] for s in spans),'textheight',9*72)\nprint('Page8 last text',[(s['text'],s['bbox']) for s in spans[-8:]])\npage.get_pixmap(matrix=pymupdf.Matrix(1.2,1.2)).save('/tmp/paper_page8.png')\nPY\ncurl -fsSLG --max-time 30 --data-urlencode 'query=DOI:10.1016/j.neuroimage.2017.06.061' --data 'format=json' --data 'resultType=core' https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search -o /tmp/varoq_original_abstract.json; jq '.resultList.result[0]|{title,authorString,abstractText,pmid}' /tmp/varoq_original_abstract.json\"","aggregated_output":"Page 8 body bounds 84.26844787597656 528.8507080078125 textheight 648\nPage8 last text [('that', (367.226806640625, 507.93011474609375, 382.1706848144531, 517.8927001953125)), (' ', (382.1706848144531, 507.93011474609375, 385.4184875488281, 517.8927001953125)), ('bounded', (385.4184875488281, 507.93011474609375, 419.7296447753906, 517.8927001953125)), (' ', (419.7296447753906, 507.93011474609375, 422.9874267578125, 517.8927001953125)), ('reporting', (422.9874267578125, 507.93011474609375, 459.51025390625, 517.8927001953125)), (' ', (459.51025390625, 507.93011474609375, 462.7680358886719, 517.8927001953125)), ('discipline,', (462.7680358886719, 507.93011474609375, 504.0032043457031, 517.8927001953125)), ('not a general guarantee for adaptive optimization or deployment on new question populations.', (108.0, 518.8881225585938, 482.73309326171875, 528.8507080078125))]\n{\n  \"title\": \"Cross-validation failure: Small sample sizes lead to large error bars.\",\n  \"authorString\": \"Varoquaux G.\",\n  \"abstractText\": \"Predictive models ground many state-of-the-art developments in statistical brain image analysis: decoding, MVPA, searchlight, or extraction of biomarkers. The principled approach to establish their validity and usefulness is cross-validation, testing prediction on unseen data. Here, I would like to raise awareness on error bars of cross-validation, which are often underestimated. Simple experiments show that sample sizes of many neuroimaging studies inherently lead to large error bars, eg±10% for 100 samples. The standard error across folds strongly underestimates them. These large error bars compromise the reliability of conclusions drawn with predictive models, such as biomarkers or methods developments where, unlike with cognitive neuroimaging MVPA approaches, more samples cannot be acquired by repeating the experiment across many subjects. Solutions to increase sample size must be investigated, tackling possible increases in heterogeneity of the data.\",\n  \"pmid\": \"28655633\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
